WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
Hello everyone, and welcome to the LangFocus Channel.

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:06.940
Today I'm going to talk about a question that I've been getting quite a lot recently,

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:10.880
and that is "What kind of job can I do with my foreign language skills?"

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.900
or "What kind of job should I do if I'm interested in foreign languages?"

00:00:15.219 --> 00:00:18.580
Now, this is a question that I respect,
because I've witnessed a lot of people

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:22.119
investing years of their life into
studying a language, kind of with the

00:00:22.119 --> 00:00:25.720
assumption that it would naturally
improve their career or give them new

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:29.439
opportunities, but without having a plan
for how they would use that language,

00:00:29.439 --> 00:00:32.839
they didn't really benefit from it and
ended up being quite frustrated.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.040
So young people who are asking this question

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.770
early on, I think you are thinking about
things the right way. But the answer to

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:42.600
this question depends on just how you
imagine your life in the future and how

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.129
far you really want to go with your
language study. Now, some jobs are

00:00:46.129 --> 00:00:50.820
specifically language-related; other jobs
are not specifically-language related,

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.539
but a foreign language could be an
asset; and for other jobs, it's not really

00:00:54.539 --> 00:00:58.539
necessary at all, but it might be useful
for your overall lifestyle. I'm going to

00:00:58.539 --> 00:01:02.340
talk about those three types of
situations. So first up are jobs that are

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:06.130
specifically language-related. For these
jobs, you need to have some expertise in

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:09.520
the language; you need to have a deep
understanding of the language of the language.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.170
So, the first kind of job is to teach a
language. Here in Japan, I know a lot of

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:16.890
people who teach English; I also know
people who teach other languages, like

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:18.450
French or Spanish.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:21.420
Now, even if you don't have a lot of
qualifications, there are some

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:24.950
opportunities to teach your language,
especially if it's a major global

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:28.820
language, and especially if it's English.
But the more qualifications you have,

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:32.570
then, of course, the better opportunities
you get and the higher pay you can get,

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:36.710
the better positions you can find. Now, if
you want to study a foreign language

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:40.049
that's not your native language and then
teach that language, then you're looking

00:01:40.049 --> 00:01:43.860
at a situation where you really need to
get some expertise; you really need to

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:47.860
get qualifications. You should get a
master's degree or maybe a PhD or

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:52.009
qualifications that show that you know
that language very well at an academic

00:01:52.009 --> 00:01:55.590
level, at a deep level, not just a
conversational level. That's if you're

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:59.259
going to teach a language that's not
your native language. Now, another type of

00:01:59.259 --> 00:02:03.899
job that is language-specific is
becoming a translator or an interpreter. Now,

00:02:03.899 --> 00:02:07.310
the difference between those, just to
clarify, a translator is somebody who

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:11.200
translates texts; they translate written
materials from one language to another;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.900
but an interpreter is somebody who
translates speech; they translate the

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:19.280
things people are saying live, in real
time, as they are speaking. Now, being a

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.330
translator or interpreter requires you
to have a very deep understanding of

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:27.540
that language, because there's really no
room for error or misunderstanding. You

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:31.000
have to translate, sometimes, very
important information, and mistakes could

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.390
be a big problem, so you need to have
very good, almost native-level skills,

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:39.910
especially if you're interpreting in real time, but
also, you need to learn the skills of

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:43.519
translation or interpreting. Those are
separate skills that you need to learn

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:46.989
on top of knowing the language. So, for
these jobs that are specifically

00:02:46.989 --> 00:02:50.781
language-related, there's really no room
for language dabblers. You really need

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:54.739
to have a comprehensive understanding of
that language, and you have to be able to

00:02:54.739 --> 00:02:59.030
show that you can use that language at
almost a native level. Next up are jobs

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:02.659
that are not specifically language
related; they are basically focused on

00:03:02.659 --> 00:03:06.980
another skill that you need, but the
language is an additional asset. The

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.860
first kind of job is one that requires
some technical expertise, but having a

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:15.010
language on top of that will give you
some extra opportunities; so an example

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:19.370
that I've heard is from an engineer. His
company sells some sort of machines to

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:23.440
companies around the world, and they send
this engineer to those companies so he

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:27.879
can teach people how to use those machines in their factories. So, he learns

00:03:27.879 --> 00:03:31.450
foreign languages so that he can do
that. I believe this guy in particular

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:35.680
was working in Indonesia, so he learned
Indonesian to be able to teach those

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:39.769
people in their native language. Another
example career is business with a

00:03:39.769 --> 00:03:44.709
regional focus; for example, maybe you get
a commerce degree, or you get a Masters

00:03:44.709 --> 00:03:49.379
of Business Administration, an MBA, but also you have some language skills, and maybe

00:03:49.379 --> 00:03:52.769
you work for a company that does
business in that region or does business

00:03:52.769 --> 00:03:56.569
with companies in that region. Another
example career is the foreign service --

00:03:56.569 --> 00:04:01.200
being a diplomat, being someone who works
at your country's embassies abroad. When

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.030
you work in the foreign service, I think
that often involves some kind of

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:07.989
language training before you are sent
abroad, but knowing some languages in

00:04:07.989 --> 00:04:12.739
advance before you apply is probably a
big asset. Another example is being a

00:04:12.739 --> 00:04:16.989
tour guide. Now, a tour guide's main focus
is not the language; it's the history and it's the

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:21.259
anthropology and all of those sorts of
things, but having in language that they

00:04:21.259 --> 00:04:25.090
can explain things in, that will help
them have other opportunities, so maybe

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:29.229
you might specialize in tourists from
Japan. You learn Japanese; you can be a

00:04:29.229 --> 00:04:32.669
tour guide for Japanese tourists.
Depending on where you live,

00:04:32.669 --> 00:04:36.850
another option might be to do some other
job in the tourism industry or open a

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:41.320
business in the tourism industry. If you
live on Bali Island in Indonesia, then you

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:45.199
would be crazy not to learn English and
Japanese and maybe some other languages

00:04:45.199 --> 00:04:49.440
that are common amongst tourists. Another
career is being a cabin attendant. Now, their

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:53.400
main focus is on safety training, on
emergency response and that kind of

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:57.509
thing, but knowing a language, that's an
additional asset; probably, they need to

00:04:57.509 --> 00:05:01.449
know English in most cases, but they also
need to know the language of the country

00:05:01.449 --> 00:05:05.509
they're working in or of the
destination that they often fly to. Maybe

00:05:05.509 --> 00:05:08.500
they don't need to know it fluently, but
knowing some of that language will help

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:12.229
them deal with the passengers on the
plane; so if you live in Los Angeles and

00:05:12.229 --> 00:05:16.220
you often fly to Asia, then knowing
Japanese or Korean or Mandarin or

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:20.120
another language like that would
probably be a big asset. Now all of these

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:24.030
jobs I just talked about, they're focused
on another important skill, so they're

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:27.970
not focused on language, so you don't
need a specific language qualification

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:31.419
in most cases, but you need to be able to
demonstrate that you can use that

00:05:31.419 --> 00:05:35.030
language on the job. that's the most
important thing. The third type of job I

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:38.570
want to mention is one that doesn't
really have any language requirement, but

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:42.470
learning that language would be useful
for your off-time and for your overall

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:46.139
lifestyle. So let's say that you live in
Singapore, and your company does business

00:05:46.139 --> 00:05:50.460
in English, then you don't have to learn
any other language; people in Singapore

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:54.680
speak English, but nearby is Malaysia and
Indonesia; so let's say you want to

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:58.740
travel there on your off time. learning
Malay or learning Indonesian would be very

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:02.539
useful in that situation, so think about
the kind of lifestyle that you want to

00:06:02.539 --> 00:06:06.060
have, and think about how languages fit
in with that. Let's say that you want to

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:10.050
live the corporate expat lifestyle in
some place like Hong Kong. Then, you can

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:14.130
study Cantonese while you get your
finance degree, or you get your MBA or

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:17.520
whatever. If you want to be a programmer,
and you can program from your laptop

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:21.230
anywhere in the world, then think about
what country you would like to live in, and

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:24.630
learn the language of that country. If
you're an avid traveler, and you love the

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:28.130
idea of living in different countries
all the time, then you can learn how to

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:31.920
do businesses on the Internet, and you
can learn the language of whatever

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:35.730
countries that you want to spend an
extended period of time in. Or if you want

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:39.370
to learn a foreign language, and you want
to someday reach an expert level and be

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:43.100
able to teach that language, then plan
ahead for that; maybe go for your master's

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:47.690
degree or PhD. Try to really get a deep
understanding and overall knowledge of

00:06:47.690 --> 00:06:51.180
that language. So these are just my
thoughts on it, based on things that I

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:54.330
know and things that I've heard, but a
lot of the viewers have different

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:58.070
perspectives. Some of you probably have
jobs that involve foreign languages, so

00:06:58.070 --> 00:07:01.640
we want to know what you think. If you
have any other ideas or any questions

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:02.710
about this topic,

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:05.990
leave them down below. Thank you for
watching, and have a nice day.

